---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
const phone = "+17044696658";
const phoneDisplay = "+1 (704) 469-6658";
---
<Layout title="Frozen AC Coils Gastonia NC | Ice on AC Unit | acrepairgastonianc.com" description="Frozen AC coils in Gastonia? Restricted airflow or refrigerant leak. Turn off the system and call for emergency repair. +1 (704) 469-6658.">
  <section class="page-hero">
    <div class="container">
      <p class="breadcrumb"><a href="/">Home</a> / <a href="/problems/">Problems</a> / Frozen Coils</p>
      <h1>Frozen Coils — Gastonia AC Problem</h1>
      <p>Ice on your indoor or outdoor unit means restricted airflow or a refrigerant leak. Running a frozen AC causes compressor damage.</p>
      <a href={`tel:${phone}`} class="btn btn-white btn-lg">Emergency Service — {phoneDisplay}</a>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="section">
    <div class="container content-block">
      <p><span class="badge badge-danger">Urgent — Act Now</span></p>
      <p>Ice on the evaporator (indoor) coil or refrigerant lines is a clear warning. Common causes: clogged air filter, blocked return vents, failing blower motor, or low refrigerant from a leak. When the coil freezes, airflow drops further and the compressor can be damaged if the system keeps running.</p>
      <h2>What to Do</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>Turn the system off at the thermostat (and breaker if needed)</li>
        <li>Do not chip ice off the coil</li>
        <li>Allow the system to thaw; check and replace the filter if dirty</li>
        <li>Call for professional diagnosis — especially if ice returns after restart</li>
      </ul>
      <p>In Gastonia humidity, frozen coils often point to refrigerant issues that need proper leak detection and repair, not just a filter change. See also: <a href="/services/refrigerant-leak-repair/">Refrigerant Leak Repair</a> and <a href="/services/emergency-ac-repair/">Emergency AC Repair</a>.</p>
      <div class="cta-box">
        <a href={`tel:${phone}`} class="btn btn-primary btn-lg">Call {phoneDisplay}</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</Layout>
